+// REX_CLAMP_RCP_RSQ: clamp +-INF from rcp/rsq to +-FLT_MAX.
+//
+// The plain rcp/rsq opcodes are translated straight to OpFDiv / InverseSqrt, so
+// a zero operand yields IEEE infinity - which is what the Xenos ALU produces
+// too. The divergence is DOWNSTREAM: on Xenos a multiply of 0 by that infinity
+// yields 0, while IEEE yields NaN. NHL's edge-AA shader does exactly that
+// (rsq of a squared edge-direction length, then multiplying the direction by
+// it), and a NaN there poisons the texture coordinates it derives. Clamping to
+// FLT_MAX keeps 0 * result == 0 while staying huge for every other input.
+// The rcpc/rsqc/rcpf variants already clamp this way.
+spv::Id SpirvShaderTranslator::ClampInfinitiesIfRequested(spv::Id result) {
+  static const bool clamp_inf = std::getenv("REX_CLAMP_RCP_RSQ") != nullptr;
